Deleting a Dockerfile will NOT stop your running containers.

This surprises many beginners in DevOps.

You might think:

“If the Dockerfile is gone, the container should break… right?”

Nope.

Here’s why 👇

Containers don’t run from the Dockerfile.

They run from the Docker image that was built using that Dockerfile.

The real flow looks like this:

Dockerfile
→ docker build
→ Docker Image
→ docker run
→ Container

Once the image is created and the container is running:

✅ The container keeps running normally
✅ The image still exists locally
❌ But you cannot rebuild the image again without the Dockerfile

Because the Dockerfile is the blueprint that tells Docker how the image was built.

That’s why in real DevOps environments:

• Dockerfiles are stored in Git repositories
• CI/CD pipelines automatically rebuild images
• Infrastructure stays reproducible and traceable

A simple rule I follow:

📦 Image = Artifact
📄 Dockerfile = Blueprint
🚀 Container = Running instance
